            <ArticleTitle>Methylene Blue for Bronchopleural Fistula Localization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>63-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/LBR.0000000000000423</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A bronchopleural fistula (BPF) is a communication between the pleural space and the bronchial tree. BPFs are challenging to diagnose and are associated with a high morbidity and mortality. Sequential balloon occlusion is commonly used for localization of a BPF. We describe our experience with 4 cases of successful localization of the BPF by instillation of methylene blue into the pleural space through a pigtail catheter, with simultaneous bronchoscopic visualization of dye in the tracheobronchial tree. Two patients were treated with endobronchial valves and 3 had a surgical thoracic muscle flap placed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
